{"subjectId":"c","id":"bitwise","title":"Bitwise Operators & Bit Tricks","group":"Types, Variables & Operators","order":11,"html":"<p class=\"lead\">C lets you manipulate individual bits — the basis of flags, masks, hardware registers, compression, hashing and many interview puzzles.</p>\n<div class=\"table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr><th>Operator</th><th>Name</th><th>Example (a = 12 = <code>1100</code>, b = 10 = <code>1010</code>)</th><th>Result</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><code>a &amp; b</code></td><td>AND</td><td><code>1100 &amp; 1010</code></td><td><code>1000</code> = 8</td></tr><tr><td><code>a | b</code></td><td>OR</td><td><code>1100 | 1010</code></td><td><code>1110</code> = 14</td></tr><tr><td><code>a ^ b</code></td><td>XOR</td><td><code>1100 ^ 1010</code></td><td><code>0110</code> = 6</td></tr><tr><td><code>~a</code></td><td>NOT (one’s complement)</td><td><code>~12</code></td><td>−13 (for <code>int</code>)</td></tr><tr><td><code>a &lt;&lt; n</code></td><td>left shift</td><td><code>12 &lt;&lt; 1</code></td><td>24 (× 2ⁿ)</td></tr><tr><td><code>a &gt;&gt; n</code></td><td>right shift</td><td><code>12 &gt;&gt; 2</code></td><td>3 (÷ 2ⁿ for non-negative)</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<figure data-lang=\"c\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>C</figcaption><pre><code>#include &lt;stdio.h&gt;\n\nvoid print_bits(unsigned x, int width) {\n    for (int i = width - 1; i &gt;= 0; i--)\n        putchar((x &gt;&gt; i) &amp; 1u ? '1' : '0');\n    putchar('\\n');\n}\n\nint main(void) {\n    unsigned a = 12, b = 10;\n    printf(\"%u %u %u %u %u\\n\", a &amp; b, a | b, a ^ b, a &lt;&lt; 1, a &gt;&gt; 2);\n    print_bits(a, 8);\n    print_bits(~a &amp; 0xFFu, 8);\n    return 0;\n}</code></pre></figure><figure data-lang=\"text\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>Output</figcaption><pre><code>8 14 6 24 3\n00001100\n11110011</code></pre></figure>\n<section class=\"block\"><h3><i class=\"ti ti-toggle-left\"></i>Set, clear, toggle, test</h3>\n<figure data-lang=\"c\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>C</figcaption><pre><code>#include &lt;stdio.h&gt;\n\n#define BIT(n) (1u &lt;&lt; (n))\n\nint main(void) {\n    unsigned flags = 0;\n    flags |= BIT(3);              /* set bit 3     */\n    flags |= BIT(0);              /* set bit 0     */\n    printf(\"%u\\n\", flags);        /* 1001 = 9      */\n    flags &amp;= ~BIT(0);             /* clear bit 0   */\n    flags ^= BIT(1);              /* toggle bit 1  */\n    printf(\"%u\\n\", flags);        /* 1010 = 10     */\n    printf(\"bit 3 is %s\\n\", (flags &amp; BIT(3)) ? \"set\" : \"clear\");\n    return 0;\n}</code></pre></figure><figure data-lang=\"text\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>Output</figcaption><pre><code>9\n10\nbit 3 is set</code></pre></figure>\n</section><section class=\"block\"><h3><i class=\"ti ti-flag\"></i>Flags as a set of options</h3>\n<figure data-lang=\"c\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>C</figcaption><pre><code>#include &lt;stdio.h&gt;\n\nenum Perm { READ = 1 &lt;&lt; 0, WRITE = 1 &lt;&lt; 1, EXEC = 1 &lt;&lt; 2 };\n\nint main(void) {\n    unsigned perms = READ | WRITE;\n    printf(\"can write? %s\\n\", (perms &amp; WRITE) ? \"yes\" : \"no\");\n    printf(\"can exec?  %s\\n\", (perms &amp; EXEC) ? \"yes\" : \"no\");\n    perms |= EXEC;\n    printf(\"rwx = %u\\n\", perms);\n    return 0;\n}</code></pre></figure><figure data-lang=\"text\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>Output</figcaption><pre><code>can write? yes\ncan exec?  no\nrwx = 7</code></pre></figure>\n</section><section class=\"block\"><h3><i class=\"ti ti-wand\"></i>Classic bit tricks</h3>\n<figure data-lang=\"c\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>C</figcaption><pre><code>#include &lt;stdio.h&gt;\n\nint is_power_of_two(unsigned x) { return x &amp;&amp; !(x &amp; (x - 1)); }\n\nint popcount(unsigned x) {          /* Kernighan: clear lowest set bit each step */\n    int n = 0;\n    while (x) { x &amp;= x - 1; n++; }\n    return n;\n}\n\nint main(void) {\n    printf(\"%d %d %d\\n\", is_power_of_two(64), is_power_of_two(96), is_power_of_two(0));\n    printf(\"popcount(255) = %d, popcount(96) = %d\\n\", popcount(255), popcount(96));\n    printf(\"lowest set bit of 96 = %u\\n\", 96u &amp; -96u);\n    printf(\"odd? %d %d\\n\", 7 &amp; 1, 10 &amp; 1);\n\n    int x = 5, y = 9;\n    x ^= y; y ^= x; x ^= y;         /* XOR swap (a curiosity; use a temp in real code) */\n    printf(\"x=%d y=%d\\n\", x, y);\n\n    int nums[] = {4, 1, 2, 1, 2};   /* every number twice except one */\n    int single = 0;\n    for (int i = 0; i &lt; 5; i++) single ^= nums[i];\n    printf(\"single = %d\\n\", single);\n    printf(\"builtin popcount = %d\\n\", __builtin_popcount(255u));\n    return 0;\n}</code></pre></figure><figure data-lang=\"text\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>Output</figcaption><pre><code>1 0 0\npopcount(255) = 8, popcount(96) = 2\nlowest set bit of 96 = 32\nodd? 1 0\nx=9 y=5\nsingle = 4\nbuiltin popcount = 8</code></pre></figure>\n<div class=\"table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr><th>Trick</th><th>Expression</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Is power of two</td><td><code>x &amp;&amp; !(x &amp; (x - 1))</code></td></tr><tr><td>Clear lowest set bit</td><td><code>x &amp; (x - 1)</code></td></tr><tr><td>Isolate lowest set bit</td><td><code>x &amp; -x</code> (unsigned)</td></tr><tr><td>Multiply / divide by 2ⁿ</td><td><code>x &lt;&lt; n</code>, <code>x &gt;&gt; n</code> (unsigned)</td></tr><tr><td>Check odd</td><td><code>x &amp; 1</code></td></tr><tr><td>Swap without temp</td><td><code>a ^= b; b ^= a; a ^= b;</code> (breaks if <code>a</code> and <code>b</code> alias!)</td></tr><tr><td>Round up to multiple of 8</td><td><code>(x + 7) &amp; ~7u</code></td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n</section><section class=\"block\"><h3><i class=\"ti ti-alert-triangle\"></i>Shift pitfalls</h3>\n<ul><li>Shifting by ≥ the width of the type (<code>1u &lt;&lt; 32</code> for a 32-bit <code>unsigned</code>) is <strong>undefined</strong>.</li><li>Left-shifting a negative value, or shifting a <code>1</code> into the sign bit of a signed <code>int</code> (<code>1 &lt;&lt; 31</code>), is <strong>undefined</strong> — use unsigned types for bit work.</li><li>Right-shifting a negative signed value is implementation-defined (usually arithmetic, copying the sign bit).</li></ul>\n<figure data-lang=\"c\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>C — oversized shift (caught by UBSan)</figcaption><pre><code>#include &lt;stdio.h&gt;\n\nint main(void) {\n    int shift = 32;\n    unsigned x = 1u &lt;&lt; shift;     /* UB: shift &gt;= width of unsigned int */\n    printf(\"%u\\n\", x);\n    return 0;\n}</code></pre></figure>\n<figure data-lang=\"text\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>UBSan</figcaption><pre><code>main.c:5:21: runtime error: shift exponent 32 is too large for 32-bit type 'unsigned int'</code></pre></figure>\n<details class=\"qa\"><summary>How do you check whether the k-th bit of n is set?</summary><div><p><code>(n &gt;&gt; k) &amp; 1</code> or <code>n &amp; (1u &lt;&lt; k)</code> (non-zero means set). Use unsigned operands so shifts into the top bit are well defined.</p></div></details>\n<details class=\"qa\"><summary>How do you find the element that appears once when all others appear twice?</summary><div><p>XOR all elements: <code>x ^ x == 0</code> and <code>x ^ 0 == x</code>, and XOR is commutative, so pairs cancel and only the single element remains. O(n) time, O(1) space.</p></div></details></section>"}